Final source-review correction (2026-10-12 UTC):
- Comparing timeout-worker lifecycle directly with `RequestFutureHolder.shutdown` exposed that the worker also must stop when the last user producer unregisters, even while a shared runtime remains active. The internal runtime producer is excluded from this owner count, matching Java's `DefaultMQProducerImpl` registration set.
- Added a lifecycle assertion first; `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core request_reply_timeout_worker_is_shared_and_joined_with_runtime --locked` failed on the new last-producer assertion before the implementation change, then passed after the fix. The same test verifies the worker remains shared while another user producer exists and restarts for a later producer.
- Reran `cargo test -p rocketmq-classic-core --locked` successfully after the fix. Strict all-target/all-feature Clippy for core and producer, `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, and `git diff --check` also pass after the fix.
- The earlier successful Maven verification remains applicable: the lifecycle correction changes only Rust runtime code and its unit test; Java fixture inputs and generated hashes are unchanged.
